		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=39259#p39259"><![CDATA[
Thanks guys. I had a feeling that solving on the velocity level would be less accurate but I'm still hazy on how exactly. From what I understand, we accumulate forces the same way, but instead of correcting these with constraint forces, we disregard the acceleration constraints and defer our correction until we've integrated the forces into a change in velocity, where we then calculate and apply our corrective impulses. But how would this practically affect the results?<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=12149">Himura78</a> — Thu Apr 27, 2017 3:38 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
